; editor - a graphical text editor. An ordinary userland program with a window.
;
;   +---------------------------------------------------+
;   | editor  /home/notes.txt  *mod*  [Save][Open][Quit] |
;   | the quick brown fox                                |
;   | jumped over the lazy dog|                          |
;   |                                                    |
;   | ln 2, col 25   saved                               |
;   +---------------------------------------------------+
;
; It creates one surface and draws into it, and knows nothing about which window
; manager is running: under desktop.cat it is a page, under tile.cat it is a tile
; that gets resized to fit. It asks the kernel only the three questions a client
; can ask about itself:
;
;   wconfig  how big am I right now?      -> re-lay the text area out
;   winput   where is the pointer on me?  -> in MY coordinates, so a click is
;                                            arithmetic, not screen geometry
;   wkey     what has been typed at me?   -> only while the manager says I am
;                                            the focused window
;
; The keyboard half of that is the part a client could not do before: a key carries
; no coordinates, so unlike the pointer there is nothing for the kernel to route it
; by, and the console line discipline - echoed, line-buffered, and already being
; read by the shell - is no use to a program that needs to see Backspace and the
; arrows as they happen. So the window manager takes the whole keyboard and hands
; each key to whichever window it calls focused, and we drain ours with wkey. Which
; window that is remains entirely the manager's business: tile.cat gives the keys
; to the last window clicked, desktop.cat to the page you are looking at.
;
; Each one arrives as the key event word sys.cat describes, and this program is the
; worked example of why it carries two views of the same keystroke. Text comes from
; the character field: the keyboard driver already knows that SHIFT+2 is '@' on this
; layout, and an editor has no business owning a second copy of that table. Keys that
; are not text - the arrows, Backspace, F2 - are matched on the raw keycode, because
; they have no character to be. And the modifier field turns the same arrow into two
; commands: SHIFT+Left extends a selection where Left just moves.
;
; Releases are ignored here. They exist for programs that need to know what is being
; *held* - a game, not an editor - and they are easy to ignore precisely because a
; release compares equal to no keycode at all.
;
;
; -- Buttons ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The right-hand end of the title bar carries one button per thing this editor can
; be told to do: [Save], [Open] and [Quit]. They are drawn there rather than listed
; as a reminder of which keys do what, because a reminder is worth exactly as much
; as the memory of having read it - and this program already knows where the pointer
; is, in its own coordinates, which is the whole of what a button needs. The keys
; still do the same three things (F2, F3, ESC); a button is the visible half of a
; key, never a second implementation of it, so both go through the same routine and
; the two can never disagree - clicking [Quit] with unsaved changes asks the same
; question ESC does, and answers it the same way.
;
; A window too narrow for the group loses it entirely (btnshow), which is the same
; rule the right-aligned key hint used to follow. The group is drawn last and paints
; its own background, so a long path simply runs underneath it instead of tangling
; with the labels.
;
;
; -- Opening another file -----------------------------------------------------
;
; [Open] and F3 put a one-line prompt in the status bar, prefilled with the file
; already being edited, since the next file you want is usually the one beside it.
; Enter loads what has been typed, ESC (or an empty line) gives up, and while the
; prompt is up it takes every key - there is nothing else sensible to do with a
; keystroke aimed at a box you are typing into.
;
; Loading replaces the buffer outright and there is no undo, so unsaved changes cost
; a second Enter, exactly as they cost a second ESC to quit. A file that will not fit
; is refused with the buffer and the path untouched, so a mistyped name is a status
; line rather than a lost afternoon; a name that does not exist is not a failure at
; all, it is how a new file is started.
;
;
; -- Settings, in /conf/editor.conf --------------------------------------------
;
; Two things about how the text is shown are matters of taste rather than of
; behaviour, so they are read out of a file instead of being compiled in:
; whether a line-number gutter is drawn, and whether a long line wraps. The
; format is the one /apps entries already use - "key=value" a line at a time,
; '#' and blank lines ignored, unknown keys skipped - because a second
; configuration syntax on one machine would be one too many.
;
; It is read once, at startup. A missing file, an unreadable one or a value that
; is not "on" leaves the setting off, which is exactly the editor there was
; before the file existed; nothing here can fail in a way that stops you editing.
;
; The gutter costs columns rather than pixels: it is as wide as the file's
; largest line number plus a blank column, and everything downstream measures the
; text area in the columns left over, so the two settings compose without either
; knowing about the other.
;
;
; -- The buffer ---------------------------------------------------------------
;
; One flat array of bytes with lines separated by '\n', a byte offset for the
; cursor, and another for the selection anchor. Inserting shifts the tail up a byte
; and deleting shifts it down, which is O(n) per keystroke and completely fine at
; this size - a 32 KB buffer moves in well under a frame. Nothing else is cached:
; the line and column under the cursor, and the number of lines, are recomputed by
; one scan of the buffer per redrawn frame, and a frame is only drawn - and only
; committed - when something has actually changed, so an idle editor costs the
; window manager nothing to show.
;
; The file is read in whole and written out whole. TEXT_MAX is under the 69632-byte
; ceiling CatFS puts on a single file, so a file that loads can always be saved.
;
;
; -- Keys ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;
;   printable      insert (replacing the selection, if there is one)
;   SHIFT+key      the capital or the other character on that key
;   Enter / Tab    newline / four spaces
;   Backspace      delete the selection, or the character before the cursor
;   Delete         delete the selection, or the character under the cursor
;   arrows         move; up/down keep the column you were aiming for
;   Home / End     start / end of line
;   PgUp / PgDn    a screenful
;   SHIFT+any move extend the selection instead of dropping it
;   F2             save
;   F3             open another file (Enter to load, ESC to give up)
;   ESC            quit (twice, if there are unsaved changes)
;
; The pointer places the caret, and dragging selects. Typing over a selection
; replaces it. A click in the title bar is a button press and never a caret.
;
;   $ bg editor /home/notes.txt
;   $ bg tile
#include "lib/sys.cat"

#define WANT_W, 400                  ; the size we would like
#define WANT_H, 300
#define TEXT_MAX, 32768              ; the largest file we will open
#define MAX_WIN_BYTES, 786432        ; 512 * 384 * 4 - any size we could be granted
#define ARENA_BYTES, 819200          ; MAX_WIN_BYTES + TEXT_MAX, in one sbrk

#define GW, 8                        ; the console font is 8x16, 1bpp
#define GH, 16
#define FONT_GLYPHS, 129
#define TABSTOP, 4                   ; Tab inserts this many spaces
#define PATH_MAX, 128                ; the longest name the open prompt will take
#define CONFBUF_MAX, 4096            ; the most of /conf/editor.conf we will read.
                                     ; Generous on purpose: the settings are at the
                                     ; end of a file that explains them, so a buffer
                                     ; sized for the settings alone would read the
                                     ; documentation and stop

; The buttons at the right-hand end of the title bar. Every label is six glyphs
; wide - "[Save]", "[Open]", "[Quit]" - so one pitch places and hit-tests all three.
#define BTN_N, 3
#define BTN_W, 48                    ; 6 * GW
#define BTN_GAP, 8                   ; one cell between two of them
#define BTN_PITCH, 56                ; BTN_W + BTN_GAP
#define BTN_SPAN, 160                ; BTN_N * BTN_PITCH - BTN_GAP: the whole group
#define BTN_MARGIN, 4                ; and its clearance from the right-hand edge
#define BTN_SAVE, 0
#define BTN_OPEN, 1
#define BTN_QUIT, 2

; Raylib keycodes. Only the keys that produce no character need naming - anything
; that types something comes through as the event's character field instead.
#define KEY_ESCAPE, 256
#define KEY_ENTER, 257
#define KEY_TAB, 258
#define KEY_BACKSPACE, 259
#define KEY_DELETE, 261
#define KEY_RIGHT, 262
#define KEY_LEFT, 263
#define KEY_DOWN, 264
#define KEY_UP, 265
#define KEY_PAGEUP, 266
#define KEY_PAGEDOWN, 267
#define KEY_HOME, 268
#define KEY_END, 269
#define KEY_F2, 291
#define KEY_F3, 292
#define KEY_KPENTER, 335

#define BG_COLOUR, 0x001E2A38        ; the page
#define TEXT_COLOUR, 0x00E8F0F5
#define BAR_BG, 0x000E141B           ; title and status bars
#define TITLE_TEXT, 0x00FFFFFF
#define HINT_TEXT, 0x008FA6BC
#define STATUS_TEXT, 0x00C8D8E4
#define DIRTY_TEXT, 0x00E8B45A       ; the unsaved-changes marker
#define SEL_BG, 0x003A6EA5
#define CARET_COLOUR, 0x00FFD479
#define BTN_BG, 0x00243544           ; a title-bar button, and its label
#define BTN_TEXT, 0x00D8E8F4
#define GUTTER_BG, 0x00172433        ; the line-number column: a shade off the page,
#define GUTTER_TEXT, 0x006F8296      ; and dimmer than the text, since it is not it


main:
    mov [g_argc], r1
    mov [g_argv], r2
    cmp r1, 2
    jul usage_exit                   ; we edit a named file, so we need the name
    mov r1, 1
    call getarg
    mov [path], r0
    call load_conf                   ; before anything is measured or drawn: the
                                     ; settings decide how wide the text area is

    sys_sbrk ARENA_BYTES             ; pixels first, the text buffer behind them.
    cmp r0, -1                       ; the window buffer is allocated at the biggest
    je mem_exit                      ; size we could be granted, so a resize never
    mov [winbuf], r0                 ; has to reallocate
    add r0, MAX_WIN_BYTES
    mov [textptr], r0

    call load_file                   ; before the window exists, so a failure can
    cmp r0, 0                        ; still be reported on the console
    jne big_exit

    sys_wcreate WANT_W, WANT_H
    cmp r0, -1
    je win_exit
    mov [sid], r0
    call measure                     ; nlines/curline for the first frame

    .loop:
        call resync                  ; adopt whatever size we have been granted
        call handle_keys
        call handle_mouse
        mov r0, [quitreq]            ; ESC and the [Quit] button both answer here,
        cmp r0, 0                    ; having agreed with themselves about unsaved
        jne .quit                    ; changes first
        mov r0, [dirty]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .idle
        call measure                 ; where the cursor is, and how many lines
        call clamp_scroll            ; scroll to keep it on screen
        call redraw
        mov r0, 0
        mov [dirty], r0
        mov r3, [winbuf]             ; commit(sid, winbuf)
        mov r2, [sid]
        mov r1, SYS_WCOMMIT
        syscall
        .idle:
        sys_yield
        jmp .loop

    .quit:
        sys_exit 0


; Pick up the size the window manager has granted us. Only the config counter
; moving means anything changed, so this is a compare in the common case.
resync:
    sys_wconfig [sid], cfgbuf
    cmp r0, -1
    je .done                         ; surface gone; the next commit will fail too
    mov r0, cfgbuf
    add r0, 8
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; config counter
    mov r1, [lastcfg]
    cmp r0, r1
    je .done
    mov [lastcfg], r0
    mov r0, [cfgbuf]                 ; granted width
    mov [winw], r0
    mov r0, cfgbuf
    add r0, 4
    mov r0, [r0]                     ; granted height
    mov [winh], r0
    call layout
    mov r0, 1                        ; redraw at the new size
    mov [dirty], r0
    .done:
    ret


; How many characters fit in the window. Two rows go to the title and status bars,
; and everything else is derived from the size we were granted - nothing here
; assumes any particular window size.
layout:
    mov r0, [winw]
    mov r1, GW
    udiv r0, r1
    cmp r0, 1
    juge .colsok
    mov r0, 1
    .colsok:
    mov [totcols], r0                ; the whole width; the gutter takes its cut in
    call text_cols                   ; text_cols, which measure calls again whenever
                                     ; the file gains or loses a digit
    mov r0, [winh]
    mov r1, GH
    udiv r0, r1
    mov [rows], r0
    sub r0, 2
    cmp r0, 1
    jige .ok
    mov r0, 1                        ; a window too short for a text area
    .ok:
    mov [textrows], r0
    mov r0, [winw]                   ; the button group, right-aligned. It depends on
    sub r0, BTN_SPAN + BTN_MARGIN    ; the width and nothing else - it paints over
    cmp r0, 0                        ; whatever the title ran into, so the length of
    jil .nobuttons                   ; the path is none of its business
    mov [btnx], r0
    mov [btnshow], 1
    ret
    .nobuttons:
    mov [btnshow], 0                 ; too narrow for them: the keys still work
    ret


; How wide the line-number gutter is, in character cells: room for the largest
; line number in the file, and one blank column between it and the text. It is
; recomputed by measure rather than by layout, because what changes it is the file
; growing past ten, a hundred, a thousand lines - not the window.
gutter_calc:
    mov r0, [shownums]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .none
    mov r1, [nlines]
    call numdigits
    add r0, 1
    mov [gutcols], r0
    jmp .cols
    .none:
    mov r0, 0
    mov [gutcols], r0
    .cols:
    call text_cols
    ret


; The columns of text the window leaves once the gutter has had its share. Never
; zero: a window narrower than its own line numbers still gets a column of text,
; and the numbers are simply clipped by the glyph blitter.
text_cols:
    mov r0, [totcols]
    mov r1, [gutcols]
    sub r0, r1
    cmp r0, 1
    jige .ok
    mov r0, 1
    .ok:
    mov [cols], r0
    ret


; How many decimal digits r1 has -> r0, at least one.
numdigits:
    push r4                          ; digits so far
    push r5                          ; what is left of the value
    mov r4, 1
    mov r5, r1
    .l:
        cmp r5, 10
        jul .done
        mov r0, r5
        mov r1, 10
        udiv r0, r1
        mov r5, r0
        add r4, 1
        jmp .l
    .done:
    mov r0, r4
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The column up/down are aiming at, as a column that exists on the row they are
; aiming it at -> r0. It is set from the window as it was when the movement began,
; and a resize in between could otherwise send the cursor off the end of a row.
goal_col:
    mov r0, [goalcol]
    mov r1, [cols]
    sub r1, 1
    cmp r0, r1
    jule .done
    mov r0, r1
    .done:
    ret


; How many screen rows a line of r1 bytes occupies while wrapping -> r0. The
; division is deliberately not rounded up: a line whose length is an exact
; multiple of the width gets one more row than it has text, because that empty
; row is where the caret sits when it is at the end of the line, and a caret with
; nowhere to be drawn is worse than a row that looks blank.
wrap_rows:
    mov r0, r1
    mov r1, [cols]
    udiv r0, r1
    add r0, 1
    ret


; ==========================
;   Configuration
; ==========================
; /conf/editor.conf, in the same "key=value" format as an /apps entry. Read once
; at startup and never again: a setting that changed under a running editor would
; have to reflow the view mid-keystroke, and the file is edited about as often as
; the machine is rebooted.

; Read the settings file, if there is one. Everything about this is best-effort -
; no file, no read, no such key and no such value all leave the defaults standing.
load_conf:
    push r4                          ; the length argument the read syscall takes
    push r5                          ; the file's fd
    push r6                          ; bytes read so far, kept across the close
    sys_open confpath, O_READ
    cmp r0, -1
    je .done
    mov r5, r0
    mov r6, 0
    .rl:                             ; as with the file being edited, a read is
        mov r4, CONFBUF_MAX - 1      ; entitled to hand back less than it was asked
        sub r4, r6                   ; for
        cmp r4, 0
        jile .rdone
        mov r3, confbuf
        add r3, r6
        mov r2, r5
        mov r1, SYS_READ
        syscall
        cmp r0, 0
        jile .rdone
        add r6, r0
        jmp .rl
    .rdone:
    sys_close r5
    cmp r6, 0
    jile .done
    mov r1, confbuf                  ; terminate whatever we got
    add r1, r6
    mov8 [r1], 0
    call parse_conf
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Walk confbuf a line at a time, setting the flag each recognised key names.
parse_conf:
    push r4                          ; the start of the line
    push r5                          ; its '=', or 0 if it has none
    push r6                          ; its end
    mov r4, confbuf
    .line:
        mov8 r0, [r4]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .done                     ; end of the file
        mov r5, 0                    ; find the end of the line and the first '='
        mov r6, r4
        .scan:
            mov8 r0, [r6]
            cmp r0, 0
            je .haveline
            cmp r0, 10               ; \n
            je .haveline
            cmp r0, 61               ; '='
            jne .scannext
            cmp r5, 0
            jne .scannext            ; a later '=' is part of the value
            mov r5, r6
            .scannext:
            add r6, 1
            jmp .scan
        .haveline:
        mov8 r0, [r4]
        cmp r0, 35                   ; '#': a comment
        je .nextline
        cmp r5, 0
        je .nextline                 ; no '=': not a setting, and not our business

        mov r1, r4                   ; which key is it?
        mov r2, r5
        mov r3, key_lineno
        call conf_keyis
        cmp r0, 0
        je .notlineno
        mov r0, shownums
        mov [confdest], r0
        jmp .store
        .notlineno:
        mov r1, r4
        mov r2, r5
        mov r3, key_wrap
        call conf_keyis
        cmp r0, 0
        je .nextline                 ; a key we do not know: skip it, do not fail
        mov r0, wrapmode
        mov [confdest], r0

        .store:
        mov r1, r5                   ; the value: just past the '=' to the line end
        add r1, 1
        mov r2, r6
        call conf_setflag

        .nextline:
        mov r4, r6                   ; past the newline, or stop at the terminator
        mov8 r0, [r4]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .done
        add r4, 1
        jmp .line
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Is the text between r1 and r2 exactly the null-terminated key at r3? -> r0 = 1/0
conf_keyis:
    push r4                          ; cursor into the line
    push r5                          ; end of the line's key
    push r6                          ; cursor into the literal
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, r2
    mov r6, r3
    .loop:
        mov8 r0, [r6]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .keyend
        cmp r4, r5
        juge .no                     ; the line's key ran out first
        mov8 r1, [r4]
        cmp r0, r1
        jne .no
        add r4, 1
        add r6, 1
        jmp .loop
    .keyend:
    cmp r4, r5
    jne .no                          ; longer: "wrapping" is not "wrap"
    mov r0, 1
    jmp .done
    .no:
    mov r0, 0
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Set the flag at [confdest] from the value between r1 and r2. "on", "1", "true"
; and "yes" are on and everything else is off, so a value nobody has thought of
; turns the setting off rather than half on. Only "o" needs a second character to
; be sure of - "on" and "off" are the one pair that starts the same way.
conf_setflag:
    push r4                          ; cursor
    push r5                          ; end
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, r2
    .lead:
        cmp r4, r5
        juge .off                    ; nothing but blanks: off
        mov8 r0, [r4]
        cmp r0, 32
        jug .have
        add r4, 1
        jmp .lead
    .have:
    mov8 r0, [r4]
    call conf_lower
    cmp r0, 49                       ; '1'
    je .on
    cmp r0, 116                      ; 't'rue
    je .on
    cmp r0, 121                      ; 'y'es
    je .on
    cmp r0, 111                      ; 'o': on, or off
    jne .off
    add r4, 1
    cmp r4, r5
    juge .off
    mov8 r0, [r4]
    call conf_lower
    cmp r0, 110                      ; 'n'
    je .on
    .off:
    mov r0, 0
    jmp .set
    .on:
    mov r0, 1
    .set:
    mov r1, [confdest]
    mov [r1], r0
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; ASCII lower case for r0 -> r0, so "ON" and "On" say what "on" says.
conf_lower:
    cmp r0, 65                       ; 'A'
    jul .done
    cmp r0, 90                       ; 'Z'
    jug .done
    add r0, 32
    .done:
    ret


; ==========================
;   The file
; ==========================

; Load the whole file into the buffer. A file that does not exist is not an error -
; that is how you start a new one. -> r0 = 0, or -1 if it will not fit.
;
; Nothing is thrown away until the size is known to be acceptable, because this runs
; again every time [Open] names another file: a refusal has to leave the buffer we
; are already editing exactly as it was, or a typo at the prompt would cost the work
; in hand.
load_file:
    push r5                          ; fd
    push r6                          ; size on disk
    push r7                          ; bytes read so far
    sys_open [path], O_READ
    cmp r0, -1
    je .new
    mov r5, r0
    sys_seek r5, 0, SEEK_END
    mov r6, r0
    cmp r6, TEXT_MAX
    jug .toobig                      ; refuse it rather than load a truncated file
    sys_seek r5, 0, SEEK_SET
    mov r7, 0
    .rl:
        cmp r7, r6
        juge .rdone
        mov r4, r6                   ; read(fd, textptr + total, size - total)
        sub r4, r7
        mov r3, [textptr]
        add r3, r7
        mov r2, r5
        mov r1, SYS_READ
        syscall
        cmp r0, 0
        jile .rdone
        add r7, r0
        jmp .rl
    .rdone:
    mov [tlen], r7
    sys_close r5
    mov r0, msg_opened
    mov [statusmsg], r0
    mov r0, 0
    jmp .ret
    .new:
    mov r0, 0
    mov [tlen], r0                   ; an empty buffer is the new file
    mov r0, msg_new
    mov [statusmsg], r0
    mov r0, 0
    jmp .ret
    .toobig:
    sys_close r5
    mov r0, msg_toobig               ; said in the status bar for the sake of the
    mov [statusmsg], r0              ; open prompt; at startup main exits instead
    mov r0, -1
    .ret:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    ret


; Write the buffer back over the file. Sets the status line either way; the editor
; carries on running whatever happened.
save_file:
    push r5                          ; fd
    push r6                          ; bytes to write
    push r7                          ; bytes written so far
    sys_open [path], O_WRITE + O_CREATE + O_TRUNC
    cmp r0, -1
    je .fail
    mov r5, r0
    mov r6, [tlen]
    mov r7, 0
    .wl:
        cmp r7, r6
        juge .wdone
        mov r4, r6
        sub r4, r7
        mov r3, [textptr]
        add r3, r7
        mov r2, r5
        mov r1, SYS_WRITE
        syscall
        cmp r0, 0
        jile .werr
        add r7, r0
        jmp .wl
    .wdone:
    sys_close r5
    mov r0, 0
    mov [modified], r0
    mov r0, msg_saved
    mov [statusmsg], r0
    jmp .ret
    .werr:
    sys_close r5
    .fail:
    mov r0, msg_wfail
    mov [statusmsg], r0
    .ret:
    mov r0, 1
    mov [dirty], r0
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    ret


; ==========================
;   Opening another file
; ==========================
; A one-line prompt in the status bar. It is a mode rather than a window of its own:
; a client here has exactly one surface, so a dialogue would have to be a rectangle
; drawn over the text with its own hit-testing, and a line of text in a bar that
; already exists says the same thing.

; Start asking, prefilled with the file we are on. Retyping a directory to reach the
; file next to this one is the thing that makes a prompt not worth using.
open_prompt:
    push r4                          ; read cursor into the current path
    push r5                          ; the character it is on
    mov r0, 0
    mov [plen], r0
    mov [loadasked], r0
    mov r4, [path]
    .copy:
        mov r0, [plen]
        cmp r0, PATH_MAX - 1
        juge .term
        mov8 r5, [r4]
        cmp r5, 0
        je .term
        mov r1, pbuf
        add r1, r0
        mov8 [r1], r5
        add r0, 1
        mov [plen], r0
        add r4, 1
        jmp .copy
    .term:
    mov r0, [plen]
    add r0, pbuf
    mov8 [r0], 0
    mov r0, 1
    mov [prompting], r0
    mov [dirty], r0
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


close_prompt:
    mov r0, 0
    mov [prompting], r0
    mov [loadasked], r0
    mov r0, 1
    mov [dirty], r0
    ret


; Append character r1 to the line, keeping it terminated so draw_text can simply
; draw it. A full line drops what will not fit rather than truncating the name it
; already holds.
prompt_putc:
    push r4                          ; write position
    mov r4, [plen]
    cmp r4, PATH_MAX - 1
    juge .done
    mov r0, r4
    add r0, pbuf
    mov8 [r0], r1
    add r4, 1
    mov [plen], r4
    add r4, pbuf
    mov8 [r4], 0
    .done:
    pop r4
    ret


prompt_erase:
    mov r0, [plen]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done
    sub r0, 1
    mov [plen], r0
    add r0, pbuf
    mov8 [r0], 0
    .done:
    ret


; Enter at the prompt. Loading replaces the buffer outright and there is no undo, so
; unsaved changes cost a second Enter - the same bargain ESC strikes, for the same
; reason. An empty line is a change of mind rather than a file called nothing.
prompt_commit:
    mov r0, [plen]
    cmp r0, 0
    je close_prompt
    mov r0, [modified]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .go
    mov r0, [loadasked]
    cmp r0, 0
    jne .go                          ; asked twice: they mean it
    mov r0, 1
    mov [loadasked], r0
    mov [dirty], r0
    mov r0, msg_discard
    mov [statusmsg], r0
    ret
    .go:
    mov r0, [path]                   ; the path load_file reads is the one we are
    mov [prevpath], r0               ; adopting, so it has to be adopted first - and
    call adopt_path                  ; put back if the file turns out not to fit
    call load_file
    cmp r0, 0
    je .ok
    mov r0, [prevpath]               ; refused: we are still editing what we were
    mov [path], r0
    call close_prompt
    ret
    .ok:
    call close_prompt
    call reset_view
    ret


; Take the typed line as the file we are editing. It is copied into our own storage
; because [path] starts out pointing into argv, which is nobody's to write.
adopt_path:
    push r4                          ; copy position
    mov r4, 0
    .copy:
        mov r0, pbuf
        add r0, r4
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        mov r1, pathbuf
        add r1, r4
        mov8 [r1], r0
        cmp r0, 0
        je .done
        add r4, 1
        jmp .copy
    .done:
    mov r0, pathbuf
    mov [path], r0
    pop r4
    ret


; A freshly loaded buffer: the cursor at the top, nothing selected, the view at the
; origin, and none of it owed to the disk yet. measure runs here rather than waiting
; for the frame, because clamp_scroll is about to be asked where line 0 is.
reset_view:
    mov r0, 0
    mov [cur], r0
    mov [topline], r0
    mov [leftcol], r0
    mov [goalcol], r0
    mov [curcol], r0
    mov [modified], r0
    mov [quitasked], r0
    mov r0, -1
    mov [anchor], r0
    call measure
    mov r0, 1
    mov [dirty], r0
    ret


; ESC, and the [Quit] button. Both ask the same question and take the same answer,
; and either can answer the other - a confirmation belongs to the editor, not to the
; control that raised it.
ask_quit:
    mov r0, [modified]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .go                           ; nothing to lose
    mov r0, [quitasked]
    cmp r0, 0
    jne .go                          ; asked twice: they mean it
    mov r0, 1
    mov [quitasked], r0
    mov [dirty], r0
    mov r0, msg_confirm
    mov [statusmsg], r0
    ret
    .go:
    mov r0, 1
    mov [quitreq], r0
    ret


; ==========================
;   The buffer
; ==========================

; The offset of the first character of the line containing offset r1 -> r0.
line_start:
    push r4                          ; scan position
    push r5                          ; buffer base
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, [textptr]
    .back:
        cmp r4, 0
        je .done
        mov r0, r5
        add r0, r4
        sub r0, 1
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        cmp r0, 10
        je .done
        sub r4, 1
        jmp .back
    .done:
    mov r0, r4
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The offset of the newline ending the line containing offset r1 (or the end of the
; buffer, for a last line with no newline) -> r0.
line_end:
    push r4                          ; scan position
    push r5                          ; buffer base
    push r6                          ; length
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, [textptr]
    mov r6, [tlen]
    .fwd:
        cmp r4, r6
        juge .done
        mov r0, r5
        add r0, r4
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        cmp r0, 10
        je .done
        add r4, 1
        jmp .fwd
    .done:
    mov r0, r4
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The offset where line r1 begins (clamped to the end of the buffer) -> r0.
offset_of_line:
    push r4                          ; lines still to skip
    push r5                          ; scan position
    push r6                          ; length
    push r7                          ; buffer base
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, 0
    mov r6, [tlen]
    mov r7, [textptr]
    .scan:
        cmp r4, 0
        je .done
        cmp r5, r6
        juge .done
        mov r0, r7
        add r0, r5
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        add r5, 1
        cmp r0, 10
        jne .scan
        sub r4, 1
        jmp .scan
    .done:
    mov r0, r5
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; One pass over the buffer for the two things a frame needs that cannot be worked
; out locally: which line the cursor is on, and how many lines there are. Called
; once per redrawn frame, so an idle editor does not walk the buffer at all.
measure:
    push r4                          ; scan position
    push r5                          ; newlines seen
    push r6                          ; length
    push r7                          ; buffer base
    mov r4, 0
    mov r5, 0
    mov r6, [tlen]
    mov r7, [textptr]
    .scan:
        cmp r4, r6
        juge .tail
        mov r0, [cur]
        cmp r4, r0
        jne .notcur
        mov [curline], r5
        .notcur:
        mov r0, r7
        add r0, r4
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        cmp r0, 10
        jne .next
        add r5, 1
        .next:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .scan
    .tail:
    mov r0, [cur]                    ; a cursor sitting at the very end never met
    cmp r0, r6                       ; the test in the loop
    jul .lines
    mov [curline], r5
    .lines:
    add r5, 1                        ; n newlines means n+1 lines
    mov [nlines], r5
    call gutter_calc                 ; a file that has just gained a digit needs a
                                     ; wider gutter, and a column less of text
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The cursor's column within its own line -> curcol.
curcol_calc:
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_start
    mov r1, [cur]
    sub r1, r0
    mov [curcol], r1
    ret


; The column up/down aim for. Any horizontal movement or edit resets it, so walking
; down past a short line and back out again keeps the column you started from.
;
; While wrapping it is the column on the *screen*, because that is what up and down
; move by then: aiming at column 130 of a line displayed 80 columns wide would be
; aiming at somewhere no row has.
set_goal:
    call curcol_calc
    mov r0, [curcol]
    mov r1, [wrapmode]
    cmp r1, 0
    je .flat
    mov r1, [cols]
    udiv r0, r1
    mov r0, r1                       ; the remainder: where it is on its own row
    .flat:
    mov [goalcol], r0
    ret


; The selection as a half-open range -> r0 = start, r1 = end, r2 = 1 if there is
; one. An anchor equal to the cursor is a plain click, not a selection.
sel_range:
    push r4                          ; anchor
    mov r4, [anchor]
    cmp r4, 0
    jil .none
    mov r0, [cur]
    cmp r4, r0
    je .none
    jul .anchorfirst
    mov r1, r4                       ; cursor .. anchor
    mov r2, 1
    pop r4
    ret
    .anchorfirst:
    mov r1, r0                       ; anchor .. cursor
    mov r0, r4
    mov r2, 1
    pop r4
    ret
    .none:
    mov r0, 0
    mov r1, 0
    mov r2, 0
    pop r4
    ret


; What every edit has in common: the file differs from the disk, the frame differs
; from the window, the selection is gone and the goal column follows the cursor.
touch:
    mov r0, 1
    mov [modified], r0
    mov [dirty], r0
    mov r0, -1
    mov [anchor], r0
    call set_goal
    ret


; Remove [r1, r2) and leave the cursor where the text was.
delete_range:
    push r4                          ; start
    push r5                          ; end
    push r6                          ; buffer base
    push r7                          ; scan position
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, r2
    cmp r4, r5
    juge .done
    mov r6, [textptr]
    mov r7, r5
    .shift:
        mov r0, [tlen]               ; pull the tail down over the hole
        cmp r7, r0
        juge .trim
        mov r0, r6
        add r0, r7
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        mov r1, r6
        add r1, r7
        sub r1, r5
        add r1, r4
        mov8 [r1], r0
        add r7, 1
        jmp .shift
    .trim:
    mov r0, [tlen]
    sub r0, r5
    add r0, r4
    mov [tlen], r0
    mov [cur], r4
    call touch
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Delete the selection if there is one -> r0 = 1 if anything went.
delete_sel:
    push r4                          ; start
    push r5                          ; end
    call sel_range
    cmp r2, 0
    je .none
    mov r4, r0
    mov r5, r1
    mov r1, r4
    mov r2, r5
    call delete_range
    mov r0, 1
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret
    .none:
    mov r0, 0
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Insert character r1 at the cursor, replacing the selection if there is one.
insert_char:
    push r4                          ; the character
    push r5                          ; scan position
    push r6                          ; buffer base
    mov r4, r1
    call delete_sel
    mov r0, [tlen]
    cmp r0, TEXT_MAX
    juge .full
    mov r6, [textptr]
    mov r5, [tlen]
    .shift:                          ; push the tail up a byte, from the top down
        mov r0, [cur]
        cmp r5, r0
        jule .place
        mov r0, r6
        add r0, r5
        sub r0, 1
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        mov r1, r6
        add r1, r5
        mov8 [r1], r0
        sub r5, 1
        jmp .shift
    .place:
    mov r0, [cur]
    add r0, r6
    mov8 [r0], r4
    mov r0, [tlen]
    add r0, 1
    mov [tlen], r0
    mov r0, [cur]
    add r0, 1
    mov [cur], r0
    call touch
    jmp .done
    .full:
    mov r0, msg_full
    mov [statusmsg], r0
    mov r0, 1
    mov [dirty], r0
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Backspace: the selection, or the character behind the cursor.
delete_back:
    call delete_sel
    cmp r0, 1
    je .done
    mov r0, [cur]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done
    mov r1, r0
    sub r1, 1
    mov r2, r0
    call delete_range
    .done:
    ret


; Delete: the selection, or the character under the cursor.
delete_fwd:
    call delete_sel
    cmp r0, 1
    je .done
    mov r0, [cur]
    mov r1, [tlen]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .done
    mov r1, r0
    mov r2, r0
    add r2, 1
    call delete_range
    .done:
    ret


; ==========================
;   Moving about
; ==========================
; Every movement ends in one of these two, and they are where SHIFT turns moving into
; selecting. The modifiers of the event being handled, and the cursor as it stood
; before that event, are both recorded once by handle_keys, so no movement below has
; to know that selections exist: with SHIFT held the anchor is planted where the
; movement started and stays put while further moves drag the far end, and without it
; the selection is dropped exactly as it always was.
;
; The horizontal moves also re-aim the goal column that up and down follow.

after_move:
    call after_vmove
    call set_goal
    ret


after_vmove:
    mov r0, 1
    mov [dirty], r0
    mov r0, [kmods]
    and r0, KMOD_SHIFT
    cmp r0, 0
    je .drop
    mov r0, [anchor]                 ; already extending: the far end stays where it
    cmp r0, 0                        ; was planted, however many moves ago
    jige .done
    mov r0, [precur]
    mov [anchor], r0
    jmp .done
    .drop:
    mov r0, -1
    mov [anchor], r0
    .done:
    ret


move_left:
    mov r0, [cur]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done
    sub r0, 1
    mov [cur], r0
    .done:
    call after_move
    ret


move_right:
    mov r0, [cur]
    mov r1, [tlen]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .done
    add r0, 1
    mov [cur], r0
    .done:
    call after_move
    ret


move_home:
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_start
    mov [cur], r0
    call after_move
    ret


move_end:
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_end
    mov [cur], r0
    call after_move
    ret


; Up and Down move by line, or by screen row while wrapping - which is the same
; thing for every line short enough to fit, and the only sensible reading of them
; for one that is not. The two versions are kept apart rather than folded together
; because the flat one is the whole of what an unwrapped editor needs, and it is
; the one running unless the settings say otherwise.
move_up:
    mov r0, [wrapmode]
    cmp r0, 0
    jne move_up_wrap
    push r4                          ; start of this line
    push r5                          ; start of the line above
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_start
    mov r4, r0
    cmp r4, 0
    je .top
    mov r1, r4
    sub r1, 1                        ; the newline that ended the line above
    call line_start
    mov r5, r0
    mov r0, r4                       ; its length, newline excluded
    sub r0, r5
    sub r0, 1
    mov r1, [goalcol]
    cmp r1, r0
    jule .colok
    mov r1, r0                       ; shorter than the goal: land at its end
    .colok:
    add r1, r5
    mov [cur], r1
    jmp .done
    .top:
    mov r0, 0                        ; already on line 1: go to the very start
    mov [cur], r0
    .done:
    call after_vmove
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The row above: the previous slice of this same line if it has one, otherwise the
; last slice of the line before it. Landing on a row that is part of a line is
; always possible at the goal column, since a row with another row after it is a
; full one; only the last row of a line can be short.
move_up_wrap:
    push r4                          ; start of this line
    push r5                          ; the row within it, then the line above's start
    push r6                          ; the column we are aiming at
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_start
    mov r4, r0
    mov r0, [cur]
    sub r0, r4
    mov r1, [cols]
    udiv r0, r1
    mov r5, r0
    call goal_col
    mov r6, r0
    cmp r5, 0
    je .prevline
    mov r0, r5                       ; the row above, in this line
    sub r0, 1
    mov r1, [cols]
    umul r0, r1
    add r0, r4
    add r0, r6
    mov [cur], r0
    jmp .done
    .prevline:
    cmp r4, 0
    je .top
    mov r1, r4
    sub r1, 1                        ; the newline that ended the line above
    call line_start
    mov r5, r0
    mov r0, r4                       ; its length, newline excluded
    sub r0, 1
    sub r0, r5
    mov r1, [cols]
    udiv r0, r1                      ; its last row
    mov r1, [cols]
    umul r0, r1
    add r0, r5
    add r0, r6
    mov r1, r4                       ; but no further than its end
    sub r1, 1
    cmp r0, r1
    jule .set
    mov r0, r1
    .set:
    mov [cur], r0
    jmp .done
    .top:
    mov r0, 0                        ; already on the first row: the very start
    mov [cur], r0
    .done:
    call after_vmove
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


move_down:
    mov r0, [wrapmode]
    cmp r0, 0
    jne move_down_wrap
    push r4                          ; end of this line
    push r5                          ; start of the line below
    push r6                          ; end of the line below
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_end
    mov r4, r0
    mov r0, [tlen]
    cmp r4, r0
    juge .bottom
    mov r5, r4
    add r5, 1                        ; past the newline
    mov r1, r5
    call line_end
    mov r6, r0
    mov r0, r6
    sub r0, r5
    mov r1, [goalcol]
    cmp r1, r0
    jule .colok
    mov r1, r0
    .colok:
    add r1, r5
    mov [cur], r1
    jmp .done
    .bottom:
    mov [cur], r4                    ; already on the last line: go to its end
    .done:
    call after_vmove
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The row below: the next slice of this line if there is one, otherwise the first
; row of the line after it. A row exists at every multiple of the width that is not
; past the end of the line, which is the whole of the test.
move_down_wrap:
    push r4                          ; this line's start
    push r5                          ; and its end
    push r6                          ; the column we are aiming at
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_start
    mov r4, r0
    mov r1, [cur]
    call line_end
    mov r5, r0
    call goal_col
    mov r6, r0
    mov r0, [cur]
    sub r0, r4
    mov r1, [cols]
    udiv r0, r1
    add r0, 1                        ; where the row below would begin
    mov r1, [cols]
    umul r0, r1
    mov r1, r5
    sub r1, r4
    cmp r0, r1
    jug .nextline                    ; past the end: this line has no more rows
    add r0, r4
    add r0, r6
    cmp r0, r5                       ; the last row can be short
    jule .set
    mov r0, r5
    .set:
    mov [cur], r0
    jmp .done
    .nextline:
    mov r0, [tlen]
    cmp r5, r0
    juge .bottom
    mov r4, r5
    add r4, 1                        ; past the newline
    mov r1, r4
    call line_end
    mov r1, r0
    mov r0, r4
    add r0, r6
    cmp r0, r1
    jule .set2
    mov r0, r1
    .set2:
    mov [cur], r0
    jmp .done
    .bottom:
    mov [cur], r5                    ; already on the last row: go to its end
    .done:
    call after_vmove
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


page_up:
    push r4
    mov r4, [textrows]
    .l:
        cmp r4, 0
        je .done
        call move_up
        sub r4, 1
        jmp .l
    .done:
    pop r4
    ret


page_down:
    push r4
    mov r4, [textrows]
    .l:
        cmp r4, 0
        je .done
        call move_down
        sub r4, 1
        jmp .l
    .done:
    pop r4
    ret


; Scroll the view so the cursor is inside it, in both directions.
clamp_scroll:
    mov r0, [wrapmode]
    cmp r0, 0
    jne clamp_scroll_wrap
    mov r0, [curline]
    mov r1, [topline]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .below
    mov [topline], r0                ; above the view: it becomes the top line
    jmp .horiz
    .below:
    mov r1, [topline]
    mov r2, [textrows]
    add r1, r2
    cmp r0, r1
    jul .horiz
    sub r0, r2                       ; below it: it becomes the bottom line
    add r0, 1
    mov [topline], r0
    .horiz:
    call curcol_calc
    mov r0, [curcol]
    mov r1, [leftcol]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .right
    mov [leftcol], r0
    jmp .done
    .right:
    mov r1, [leftcol]
    mov r2, [cols]
    add r1, r2
    cmp r0, r1
    jul .done
    sub r0, r2
    add r0, 1
    mov [leftcol], r0
    .done:
    ret


; The same job while wrapping, where a line is worth as many rows as it needs, so
; "is the cursor on screen" is a sum rather than a subtraction. The view is still
; anchored to a whole line - there is nowhere else for topline to point - so the one
; case this cannot satisfy is a single line taller than the window: it shows that
; line from its start and stops, rather than scrolling for ever. Nothing scrolls
; sideways here, by definition.
clamp_scroll_wrap:
    call curcol_calc
    mov r0, [curline]
    mov r1, [topline]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .fit
    mov [topline], r0                ; above the view: it becomes the top line
    jmp .zero
    .fit:
        call cursor_vrow
        mov r1, [textrows]
        cmp r0, r1
        jul .zero
        mov r0, [topline]
        mov r1, [curline]
        cmp r0, r1
        juge .zero                   ; the cursor's own line fills the window
        add r0, 1
        mov [topline], r0
        jmp .fit
    .zero:
    mov r0, 0
    mov [leftcol], r0
    ret


; How many rows below the top of the view the cursor sits, while wrapping -> r0.
; Every line from topline up to the cursor's own is worth what it wraps to, and the
; cursor's own column decides which of its rows it is on. Only called for a cursor
; at or below topline, which clamp_scroll_wrap guarantees before it asks.
cursor_vrow:
    push r4                          ; line index
    push r5                          ; that line's offset
    push r6                          ; rows counted so far
    push r7                          ; that line's end
    mov r4, [topline]
    mov r1, r4
    call offset_of_line
    mov r5, r0
    mov r6, 0
    .lines:
        mov r0, [curline]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .here
        mov r1, r5
        call line_end
        mov r7, r0
        mov r1, r7
        sub r1, r5
        call wrap_rows
        add r6, r0
        mov r5, r7
        add r5, 1                    ; past the newline
        add r4, 1
        jmp .lines
    .here:
    mov r0, [curcol]
    mov r1, [cols]
    udiv r0, r1
    add r6, r0
    mov r0, r6
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; ==========================
;   Input
; ==========================

; Drain the key events the window manager has given us. We only ever see them while
; it considers us focused, and anything not recognised below is simply dropped.
;
; Each event is taken apart once: the keycode for the keys that are commands, the
; character for the keys that are text, and the modifiers - stashed in kmods, with
; the cursor's position in precur - for the movements, which read them through
; after_move.
;
; While the open prompt is up it takes the lot: a keystroke aimed at a box you are
; typing into has no other sensible reading, and an editor that went on inserting
; behind an open dialogue would be one you could not trust to be looking at what you
; are typing.
handle_keys:
    push r4                          ; keycode
    push r6                          ; the character this key types, 0 if none
    .drain:
        sys_wkey [sid]
        cmp r0, -1
        je .done                     ; nothing waiting for us
        mov r4, r0
        mov r1, r4
        and r1, KEV_UP
        cmp r1, 0
        jne .drain                   ; a release: we act on presses
        mov r6, r4
        kev_char r6
        mov r1, r4
        kev_mods r1
        mov [kmods], r1
        mov r1, r4                   ; a modifier on its own is not a keystroke: its
        and r1, KEV_MODKEY           ; state is already in every event that matters,
        cmp r1, 0                    ; and letting it through would cancel the quit
        jne .drain                   ; confirmation below. Which keycodes those are
        kev_code r4                  ; stays the keyboard driver's business
        mov r0, [prompting]          ; the open prompt takes every key while it is up
        cmp r0, 0
        jne .prompt
        mov r0, [cur]                ; where a movement would be starting from
        mov [precur], r0
        cmp r4, KEY_ESCAPE
        je .escape
        mov r0, 0                    ; any other key answers "no" to the quit
        mov [quitasked], r0          ; confirmation
        cmp r4, KEY_F2
        je .save
        cmp r4, KEY_F3
        je .open
        cmp r4, KEY_ENTER
        je .newline
        cmp r4, KEY_KPENTER
        je .newline
        cmp r4, KEY_TAB
        je .tab
        cmp r4, KEY_BACKSPACE
        je .back
        cmp r4, KEY_DELETE
        je .del
        cmp r4, KEY_LEFT
        je .left
        cmp r4, KEY_RIGHT
        je .right
        cmp r4, KEY_UP
        je .up
        cmp r4, KEY_DOWN
        je .down
        cmp r4, KEY_HOME
        je .home
        cmp r4, KEY_END
        je .end
        cmp r4, KEY_PAGEUP
        je .pgup
        cmp r4, KEY_PAGEDOWN
        je .pgdn
        cmp r6, 32                   ; not a command: if the key types something
        jul .drain                   ; printable, the driver has already told us what,
        mov r1, r6                   ; shift and all. A control chord makes a control
                                     ; code, which is not text either.
        call insert_char
        jmp .drain

        .escape:
        call ask_quit                ; which the [Quit] button also calls
        jmp .drain

        .save:
        call save_file
        jmp .drain
        .open:
        call open_prompt
        jmp .drain
        .newline:
        mov r1, 10
        call insert_char
        jmp .drain
        .tab:
        mov r0, TABSTOP              ; spaces, so the width is not a question the
        mov [tabn], r0               ; renderer has to answer
        .tabloop:
            mov r0, [tabn]
            cmp r0, 0
            je .drain
            sub r0, 1
            mov [tabn], r0
            mov r1, 32
            call insert_char
            jmp .tabloop
        .back:
        call delete_back
        jmp .drain
        .del:
        call delete_fwd
        jmp .drain
        .left:
        call move_left
        jmp .drain
        .right:
        call move_right
        jmp .drain
        .up:
        call move_up
        jmp .drain
        .down:
        call move_down
        jmp .drain
        .home:
        call move_home
        jmp .drain
        .end:
        call move_end
        jmp .drain
        .pgup:
        call page_up
        jmp .drain
        .pgdn:
        call page_down
        jmp .drain

        .prompt:                     ; the open prompt's own line editing
        cmp r4, KEY_ENTER
        je .pcommit
        cmp r4, KEY_KPENTER
        je .pcommit
        cmp r4, KEY_ESCAPE
        je .pcancel
        cmp r4, KEY_BACKSPACE
        je .prub
        cmp r6, 32                   ; a key that types nothing types nothing here
        jul .drain                   ; either: a path is made of characters
        mov r1, r6
        call prompt_putc
        .pedited:
        mov r0, 0                    ; editing the name is an answer of "no" to the
        mov [loadasked], r0          ; discard question, and takes it back
        mov r0, 1
        mov [dirty], r0
        jmp .drain
        .prub:
        call prompt_erase
        jmp .pedited
        .pcommit:
        call prompt_commit
        jmp .drain
        .pcancel:
        call close_prompt
        jmp .drain
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r4
    ret


; The pointer places the caret; holding the button and moving selects. winput gives
; it to us in our own coordinates - the window manager did the hit-testing, because
; it is the only thing that knows where we were put - so this is arithmetic.
;
; A press in the title bar is a button press and nothing else. Only a press, though:
; a drag that wanders up out of the text is not a click on whatever it ends over, and
; hit_offset already answers -1 for that row, so it simply stops moving the caret.
handle_mouse:
    push r4                          ; buttons this frame
    push r5                          ; x
    push r6                          ; y
    sys_winput [sid], inbuf
    cmp r0, -1
    je .done
    mov r5, [inbuf]
    mov r0, inbuf
    add r0, 4
    mov r6, [r0]
    mov r0, inbuf
    add r0, 8
    mov r4, [r0]
    mov r0, r4                       ; bit 0 = left button
    and r0, 1
    cmp r0, 0
    je .store
    mov r0, [prevbtn]
    and r0, 1
    cmp r0, 0
    jne .drag                        ; still held from last frame
    cmp r6, GH
    jul .titlebar                    ; a fresh press on the bar is a button, not a
    mov r1, r5                       ; caret
    mov r2, r6
    call hit_offset
    cmp r0, -1
    je .store                        ; not over the text
    mov [cur], r0                    ; press: caret here, and anchor a selection
    mov [anchor], r0
    jmp .placed
    .drag:
    mov r1, r5
    mov r2, r6
    call hit_offset
    cmp r0, -1
    je .store
    mov r1, [cur]                    ; held: drag the far end of the selection
    cmp r0, r1
    je .store
    mov [cur], r0
    .placed:
    call set_goal
    mov r0, 1
    mov [dirty], r0
    jmp .store
    .titlebar:
    mov r1, r5
    call button_hit
    .store:
    mov [prevbtn], r4
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The buffer offset under pixel (r1, r2) -> r0, or -1 if that is not the text area
; (which covers the pointer being somewhere else entirely, reported as -1, -1).
; Clicking past the end of a line lands at its end, and past the last line at the
; end of the buffer, which is what every other editor does.
; A click in the gutter is not a miss: it is the row's own line, at its start,
; which is where a click to the left of the text should land in any case. While
; wrapping, the row has to be walked to rather than added to topline, since the
; lines above it are worth as many rows as they wrap to.
hit_offset:
    push r4                          ; row, then line
    push r5                          ; x, then column
    push r6                          ; that line's offset
    push r7                          ; the line index, while walking wrapped rows
    mov r4, r2
    mov r5, r1
    cmp r4, 0
    jil .miss
    cmp r5, 0
    jil .miss
    mov r0, r4
    mov r1, GH
    udiv r0, r1
    cmp r0, 1
    jul .miss                        ; the title bar
    sub r0, 1
    mov r1, [textrows]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .miss                       ; the status bar, or below the last row
    mov r4, r0                       ; the row within the text area
    mov r0, r5
    mov r1, GW
    udiv r0, r1
    mov r1, [gutcols]
    cmp r0, r1
    jul .gutter                      ; left of the text: column 0 of this row
    sub r0, r1
    jmp .havecol
    .gutter:
    mov r0, 0
    .havecol:
    mov r5, r0
    mov r0, [wrapmode]
    cmp r0, 0
    jne .wrap

    mov r0, [topline]                ; unwrapped: one row is one line
    add r4, r0
    mov r0, [leftcol]
    add r5, r0
    mov r0, [nlines]
    cmp r4, r0
    jul .haveline
    mov r0, [tlen]                   ; past the last line
    jmp .ret
    .haveline:
    mov r1, r4
    call offset_of_line
    mov r6, r0
    mov r1, r6
    call line_end
    mov r1, r0
    mov r0, r6
    add r0, r5
    cmp r0, r1
    jule .ret
    mov r0, r1                       ; past the end of that line
    jmp .ret

    .wrap:                           ; spend the row on each line until one holds it
    mov r7, [topline]
    mov r1, r7
    call offset_of_line
    mov r6, r0
    .wl:
        mov r0, [nlines]
        cmp r7, r0
        juge .endbuf
        mov r1, r6
        call line_end
        mov [hitle], r0
        mov r1, r0
        sub r1, r6
        call wrap_rows
        cmp r4, r0
        jul .found
        sub r4, r0
        mov r6, [hitle]
        add r6, 1                    ; past the newline
        add r7, 1
        jmp .wl
    .found:
    mov r0, r4                       ; which of that line's rows, and the column on it
    mov r1, [cols]
    umul r0, r1
    add r0, r5
    add r0, r6
    mov r1, [hitle]
    cmp r0, r1
    jule .ret
    mov r0, r1                       ; past the end of that line
    jmp .ret
    .endbuf:
    mov r0, [tlen]                   ; below the last row of the last line

    .ret:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret
    .miss:
    mov r0, -1
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; A press in the title bar, at x = r1. One division places it, because every button
; is the same width and they sit on one pitch - the arithmetic that laid them out,
; run backwards. Anything else in that bar (the title, the gaps between buttons, the
; margin past the last one) is deliberately inert: a title bar is a place to read,
; and a control that fires from an inch away is worse than no control.
button_hit:
    push r4                          ; x, relative to the group
    mov r0, [btnshow]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done                         ; too narrow to have drawn them
    mov r4, [btnx]
    cmp r1, r4
    jul .done                        ; left of the group: the title
    mov r4, r1
    mov r0, [btnx]
    sub r4, r0
    mov r0, r4
    mov r1, BTN_PITCH
    udiv r0, r1                      ; r0 = which button, r1 = x inside its pitch
    cmp r1, BTN_W
    juge .done                       ; in the gap between two of them
    cmp r0, BTN_N
    juge .done                       ; past the last one, in the margin
    cmp r0, BTN_SAVE
    je .save
    cmp r0, BTN_OPEN
    je .open
    call ask_quit
    jmp .done
    .save:
    call save_file
    jmp .done
    .open:
    call open_prompt
    .done:
    pop r4
    ret


; The label on button r1 -> r0.
button_label:
    cmp r1, BTN_SAVE
    je .save
    cmp r1, BTN_OPEN
    je .open
    mov r0, msg_bquit
    ret
    .save:
    mov r0, msg_bsave
    ret
    .open:
    mov r0, msg_bopen
    ret


; ==========================
;   Drawing
; ==========================

redraw:
    call clear_win
    call draw_title
    call draw_body
    call draw_status
    ret


; The title bar: what we are editing, whether it has been changed, and the buttons
; for the three things that can be done to it.
draw_title:
    push r4                          ; pen x, after the title
    mov [fx], 0
    mov [fy], 0
    mov r0, [winw]
    mov [fw], r0
    mov [fh], GH
    mov [fc], BAR_BG
    call fill_rect
    mov [gy], 0
    mov [gc], TITLE_TEXT
    mov r1, msg_title
    mov r2, GW
    call draw_text
    mov r4, r0
    mov r1, [path]
    mov r2, r4
    call draw_text
    mov r4, r0
    mov r0, [modified]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .buttons
    mov [gc], DIRTY_TEXT
    mov r1, msg_mod
    mov r2, r4
    call draw_text
    .buttons:
    call draw_buttons
    pop r4
    ret


; The buttons, at the right-hand end of the bar. The whole group is repainted with
; the bar's own colour first, so a path long enough to reach them is cut off cleanly
; instead of showing through the labels - they are drawn last precisely so that the
; title can be allowed to overrun without anyone having to measure it.
draw_buttons:
    push r4                          ; button index
    push r5                          ; its left edge
    mov r0, [btnshow]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done
    mov r0, [btnx]
    sub r0, BTN_GAP                  ; a cell of clearance, unless the window is only
    cmp r0, 0                        ; just wide enough for the group: fill_rect
    jige .haveedge                   ; indexes from fx and cannot be given a negative
    mov r0, 0                        ; one
    .haveedge:
    mov [fx], r0
    mov [fy], 0
    mov r1, [winw]
    sub r1, r0
    mov [fw], r1
    mov [fh], GH
    mov [fc], BAR_BG
    call fill_rect
    mov [gy], 0
    mov r4, 0
    .each:
        cmp r4, BTN_N
        juge .done
        mov r5, r4
        umul r5, BTN_PITCH
        mov r0, [btnx]
        add r5, r0
        mov [fx], r5
        mov [fy], 0
        mov [fw], BTN_W
        mov [fh], GH
        mov [fc], BTN_BG
        call fill_rect
        mov [gc], BTN_TEXT
        mov r1, r4
        call button_label
        mov r1, r0
        mov r2, r5
        call draw_text
        add r4, 1
        jmp .each
    .done:
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The status bar: where the cursor is, and whatever last happened - or, while a file
; is being named, the line being typed. The prompt borrows this row rather than
; drawing a box of its own: the bar is already there, already the width of the
; window, and already the place this program says things.
draw_status:
    push r4                          ; pen x
    mov r0, [rows]
    sub r0, 1
    umul r0, GH
    mov [fy], r0
    mov [gy], r0
    mov [fx], 0
    mov r0, [winw]
    mov [fw], r0
    mov [fh], GH
    mov [fc], BAR_BG
    call fill_rect
    mov r0, [prompting]
    cmp r0, 0
    jne .prompt
    mov [gc], HINT_TEXT
    mov r1, msg_ln
    mov r2, GW
    call draw_text
    mov r4, r0
    mov r1, [curline]                ; lines and columns are 1-based to a human
    add r1, 1
    mov r2, r4
    call draw_num
    mov r4, r0
    mov r1, msg_col
    mov r2, r4
    call draw_text
    mov r4, r0
    mov r1, [curcol]
    add r1, 1
    mov r2, r4
    call draw_num
    mov r4, r0
    add r4, GW * 3
    mov [gc], STATUS_TEXT
    mov r1, [statusmsg]
    mov r2, r4
    call draw_text
    pop r4
    ret

    .prompt:
    mov [gc], TITLE_TEXT
    mov r1, msg_open
    mov r2, GW
    call draw_text
    mov r1, pbuf
    mov r2, r0
    call draw_text
    mov [fx], r0                     ; a block where the next character will go, so
    mov [fw], GW                     ; an empty line still looks like one you are
    mov [fh], GH                     ; being asked to fill in
    mov [fc], CARET_COLOUR
    call fill_rect
    pop r4
    ret


; The visible slice of the buffer, then the caret on top of it. The selection is
; resolved once for the whole pass, and the two ways of laying the text out share
; everything below the row: a row is a row whether it is a whole line or a slice
; of one.
draw_body:
    push r4                          ; screen row
    push r5                          ; offset of the line being drawn
    call sel_range                   ; one range for the whole pass
    cmp r2, 0
    jne .havesel
    mov r0, -1                       ; no selection: a range nothing falls inside
    mov r1, -1
    .havesel:
    mov [selstart], r0
    mov [selend], r1
    mov r0, [wrapmode]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .flat
    call draw_wrapped
    jmp .caret
    .flat:
    mov r1, [topline]
    call offset_of_line
    mov r5, r0
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        mov r0, [textrows]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .caret
        mov r0, [topline]
        add r0, r4
        mov r1, [nlines]
        cmp r0, r1
        juge .caret                  ; past the last line: leave the page bare
        mov r2, r0
        add r2, 1                    ; lines are 1-based to a human
        mov r1, r4
        call draw_gutter
        mov r1, r4
        mov r2, r5
        call draw_line
        mov r5, r0
        add r4, 1
        jmp .rows
    .caret:
    call draw_caret
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The visible text with wrapping on: each line spends as many rows as it needs, and
; only its first one carries a number - a continuation is not a line, and numbering
; it would say there were more lines than there are.
draw_wrapped:
    push r4                          ; screen row
    push r5                          ; offset of the row being drawn
    push r6                          ; line index
    push r7                          ; that line's end
    mov r6, [topline]
    mov r1, r6
    call offset_of_line
    mov r5, r0
    mov r4, 0
    .lines:
        mov r0, [textrows]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .done
        mov r0, [nlines]
        cmp r6, r0
        juge .done                   ; past the last line: leave the page bare
        mov r1, r5
        call line_end
        mov r7, r0
        mov r1, r7                   ; how many rows this line is worth, which is
        sub r1, r5                   ; what the caret and the hit test also count
        call wrap_rows
        mov [rowsleft], r0
        mov r0, r6
        add r0, 1
        mov [rownum], r0             ; the first row of the line, and only it
        .segs:
            mov r0, [textrows]
            cmp r4, r0
            juge .done
            mov r1, r4
            mov r2, [rownum]
            call draw_gutter
            mov r0, -1
            mov [rownum], r0
            mov r1, r4
            mov r2, r5
            mov r3, r7
            call draw_span
            mov r5, r0               ; where it stopped: the next row starts there
            add r4, 1
            mov r0, [rowsleft]
            sub r0, 1
            mov [rowsleft], r0
            cmp r0, 0
            jne .segs
        mov r5, r7
        add r5, 1                    ; past the newline
        add r6, 1
        jmp .lines
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The caret: a two-pixel bar between characters. Which row and column that is comes
; out differently in the two layouts - a sum of wrapped line heights, or a
; subtraction - but it is drawn in one place, so the two can never disagree about
; where the cursor is with the text saying otherwise.
draw_caret:
    push r4                          ; row
    push r5                          ; column
    mov r0, [curline]
    mov r1, [topline]
    cmp r0, r1
    jul .done                        ; scrolled off the top
    mov r0, [wrapmode]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .flat
    call cursor_vrow
    mov r4, r0
    mov r0, [curcol]
    mov r1, [cols]
    udiv r0, r1
    mov r5, r1                       ; the remainder: the column on its own row
    jmp .have
    .flat:
    mov r0, [curline]
    mov r1, [topline]
    sub r0, r1
    mov r4, r0
    mov r0, [curcol]
    mov r1, [leftcol]
    cmp r0, r1
    jul .done                        ; scrolled off the left
    sub r0, r1
    mov r5, r0
    .have:
    mov r0, [textrows]
    cmp r4, r0
    juge .done
    mov r0, [cols]
    cmp r5, r0
    juge .done
    mov r0, r4
    add r0, 1                        ; the title bar is row 0
    umul r0, GH
    mov [fy], r0
    mov r1, r5
    call cell_x
    mov [fx], r0
    mov [fw], 2
    mov [fh], GH
    mov [fc], CARET_COLOUR
    call fill_rect
    .done:
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The line-number column for one screen row. r1 = the row, r2 = the line number
; that belongs in it, or -1 for a row continuing a wrapped line. Draws nothing at
; all when line numbers are off, which is what keeps that setting out of every
; other routine down here.
draw_gutter:
    push r4                          ; pixel y
    push r5                          ; the number
    mov r0, [shownums]
    cmp r0, 0
    je .done
    mov r5, r2
    mov r0, r1
    add r0, 1                        ; the title bar is row 0
    umul r0, GH
    mov r4, r0
    mov [fx], 0
    mov [fy], r4
    mov r0, [gutcols]
    umul r0, GW
    mov [fw], r0
    mov [fh], GH
    mov [fc], GUTTER_BG
    call fill_rect
    cmp r5, 0
    jil .done                        ; a continuation row: the strip and nothing else
    mov r1, r5                       ; right-aligned against the blank column that
    call numdigits                   ; separates the gutter from the text
    mov r1, [gutcols]
    sub r1, 1
    sub r1, r0
    cmp r1, 0
    jige .havex
    mov r1, 0                        ; wider than its gutter: clipped where it is,
    .havex:                          ; never moved into the text
    umul r1, GW
    mov r2, r1
    mov r1, r5
    mov [gy], r4
    mov [gc], GUTTER_TEXT
    call draw_num
    .done:
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; One whole line, unwrapped. r1 = screen row, r2 = the line's offset in the buffer.
; -> r0 = the offset of the line after it.
draw_line:
    push r4                          ; screen row
    push r5                          ; the first character we can see
    push r6                          ; the line's end
    push r7                          ; the line's offset
    mov r4, r1
    mov r7, r2
    mov r1, r7
    call line_end
    mov r6, r0
    mov r0, r7                       ; skip the columns scrolled off the left
    mov r1, [leftcol]
    add r0, r1
    cmp r0, r6
    jule .fromleft
    mov r0, r6                       ; the line is shorter than the scroll
    .fromleft:
    mov r5, r0
    mov r1, r4
    mov r2, r5
    mov r3, r6
    call draw_span
    mov r0, r6
    mov r1, [tlen]
    cmp r0, r1
    juge .done
    add r0, 1                        ; step over the newline
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; One screen row of text: r1 = the row, r2 = the first character on it, r3 = where
; its logical line ends. Stops at the end of the line or at the width of the text
; area, whichever comes first, and hands back the offset it stopped at - which is
; where the next row of a wrapped line begins.
draw_span:
    push r4                          ; pixel y
    push r5                          ; offset of the character being drawn
    push r6                          ; column within the text area
    push r7                          ; offset of the line's end
    mov r0, r1
    add r0, 1                        ; the title bar is row 0
    umul r0, GH
    mov r4, r0
    mov [gy], r4
    mov r5, r2
    mov r7, r3
    mov r6, 0
    .each:
        mov r0, [cols]
        cmp r6, r0
        juge .eol
        cmp r5, r7
        juge .eol
        mov r0, [selstart]           ; selected characters sit on a filled cell
        cmp r5, r0
        jul .nosel
        mov r0, [selend]
        cmp r5, r0
        juge .nosel
        mov r1, r6
        call cell_x
        mov [fx], r0
        mov [fy], r4
        mov [fw], GW
        mov [fh], GH
        mov [fc], SEL_BG
        call fill_rect
        .nosel:
        mov r0, [textptr]
        add r0, r5
        mov8 r0, [r0]
        cmp r0, 32
        jule .skip                   ; blank, or a control byte we will not draw
        mov [gch], r0
        mov r1, r6
        call cell_x
        mov [gx], r0
        mov [gc], TEXT_COLOUR
        call draw_glyph
        .skip:
        add r5, 1
        add r6, 1
        jmp .each
    .eol:
    cmp r5, r7                       ; a selection that runs on to the next line
    jne .done                        ; shows the newline as a half-width block
    mov r0, [cols]
    cmp r6, r0
    juge .done
    mov r0, [selstart]
    cmp r7, r0
    jul .done
    mov r0, [selend]
    cmp r7, r0
    juge .done
    mov r1, r6
    call cell_x
    mov [fx], r0
    mov [fy], r4
    mov [fw], GW / 2
    mov [fh], GH
    mov [fc], SEL_BG
    call fill_rect
    .done:
    mov r0, r5
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; The pixel x of text column r1 -> r0. Everything down here counts in columns of
; text, so this is the single place the gutter shifts anything sideways.
cell_x:
    mov r0, [gutcols]
    add r0, r1
    umul r0, GW
    ret


; ==========================
;   Primitives
; ==========================

; Draw a null-terminated string. r1 = string, r2 = pen x. Uses gy and gc, and
; hands back the pen where the next string would start.
draw_text:
    push r4                          ; read cursor
    push r5                          ; pen x
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, r2
    .each:
        mov8 r0, [r4]
        cmp r0, 0
        je .done
        cmp r0, 32
        jule .skip
        mov [gch], r0
        mov [gx], r5
        call draw_glyph
        .skip:
        add r5, GW
        add r4, 1
        jmp .each
    .done:
    mov r0, r5
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Draw an unsigned number. r1 = value, r2 = pen x -> r0 = the pen after it.
draw_num:
    push r4                          ; pen x
    mov r4, r2
    mov r2, numbuf
    call itoa
    mov r1, numbuf
    mov r2, r4
    call draw_text
    pop r4
    ret


; One 8x16 glyph from the embedded console font. Inputs (globals): gx, gy (its
; top-left pixel), gch (the character), gc (the colour). Rows whose font byte is
; zero are skipped, which is most of them.
draw_glyph:
    push r4                          ; glyph row
    push r5                          ; that row's font byte
    push r6                          ; where the row lands in the buffer
    push r7                          ; bit index within the row
    mov r0, [gch]
    cmp r0, FONT_GLYPHS
    juge .done                       ; unmapped code -> draw nothing
    umul r0, GH
    add r0, font
    mov [gsrc], r0
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        cmp r4, GH
        juge .done
        mov r0, [gy]
        add r0, r4                   ; py
        mov r1, [winh]
        cmp r0, r1
        juge .done                   ; off the bottom
        mov r5, [gsrc]
        add r5, r4
        mov8 r5, [r5]                ; 1bpp row, MSB = leftmost pixel
        cmp r5, 0
        je .nextrow
        mov r1, [winw]               ; dst = winbuf + (py*winw + gx)*4
        umul r0, r1
        mov r1, [gx]
        add r0, r1
        umul r0, 4
        mov r6, [winbuf]
        add r6, r0
        mov r7, 0
        .bits:
            cmp r7, GW
            juge .nextrow
            mov r0, 7
            sub r0, r7
            mov r1, r5
            shr r1, r0
            and r1, 1
            cmp r1, 0
            je .nextbit
            mov r0, [gx]
            add r0, r7
            mov r1, [winw]
            cmp r0, r1
            juge .nextrow            ; ran off the right edge
            mov r0, r7
            umul r0, 4
            add r0, r6
            mov r1, [gc]
            mov [r0], r1
            .nextbit:
            add r7, 1
            jmp .bits
        .nextrow:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .rows
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; A solid rectangle, clipped to the window. Inputs (globals): fx, fy, fw, fh, fc.
fill_rect:
    push r4                          ; row
    push r5                          ; py
    push r6                          ; clipped width
    push r7                          ; column counter
    mov r4, 0
    .rows:
        mov r0, [fh]
        cmp r4, r0
        juge .done
        mov r5, [fy]
        add r5, r4
        cmp r5, 0
        jil .next
        mov r0, [winh]
        cmp r5, r0
        juge .done
        mov r6, [fw]
        mov r0, [fx]
        add r0, r6
        mov r1, [winw]
        cmp r0, r1
        jule .wok
        mov r6, [winw]
        mov r0, [fx]
        sub r6, r0
        .wok:
        cmp r6, 0
        jile .next
        mov r0, r5                   ; dst = winbuf + (py*winw + x)*4
        mov r1, [winw]
        umul r0, r1
        mov r1, [fx]
        add r0, r1
        umul r0, 4
        mov r2, [winbuf]
        add r0, r2
        mov r1, [fc]
        mov r7, r6
        .cols:
            mov [r0], r1
            add r0, 4
            sub r7, 1
            cmp r7, 0
            jne .cols
        .next:
        add r4, 1
        jmp .rows
    .done:
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; Fill the window with the background, by the doubling copy trick: seed one pixel,
; then repeatedly copy the filled span onto itself.
clear_win:
    push r4                          ; span already filled
    push r5                          ; remaining
    push r6                          ; total bytes
    mov r6, [winw]
    mov r0, [winh]
    umul r6, r0
    umul r6, 4
    cmp r6, 0
    je .done
    mov r0, [winbuf]
    mov [r0], BG_COLOUR
    mov r4, 4
    .fill:
        cmp r4, r6
        juge .done
        mov r5, r6
        sub r5, r4
        cmp r4, r5
        jule .use
        mov r0, [winbuf]
        add r0, r4
        mov r1, [winbuf]
        cpy r1, r5
        jmp .done
        .use:
        mov r0, [winbuf]
        add r0, r4
        mov r1, [winbuf]
        cpy r1, r4
        add r4, r4
        jmp .fill
    .done:
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; ==========================
;   Small utilities
; ==========================

; getarg(r1 = index) -> r0 = argv[index].
getarg:
    shl r1, 2
    mov r0, [g_argv]
    add r1, r0
    mov r0, [r1]
    ret


; itoa(r1 = value, r2 = buffer): unsigned decimal, null-terminated.
itoa:
    push r4                          ; value
    push r5                          ; buffer
    push r6                          ; digit count
    push r7                          ; write index
    mov r4, r1
    mov r5, r2
    mov r6, 0
    cmp r4, 0
    jne .gen
    mov8 [r5], 48                    ; "0"
    mov r6, 1
    jmp .term
    .gen:
    .gl:                             ; least significant digit first, onto the stack
        cmp r4, 0
        je .emit
        mov r0, r4
        mov r1, 10
        udiv r0, r1                  ; r0 = quotient, r1 = remainder
        add r1, 48
        push r1
        add r6, 1
        mov r4, r0
        jmp .gl
    .emit:
        mov r7, 0
    .ol:                             ; and back off it in order
        cmp r7, r6
        juge .term
        pop r1
        mov r0, r5
        add r0, r7
        mov8 [r0], r1
        add r7, 1
        jmp .ol
    .term:
    mov r0, r5
    add r0, r6
    mov8 [r0], 0
    pop r7
    pop r6
    pop r5
    pop r4
    ret


; ==========================
;   Error exits
; ==========================
; All before the window exists, so the console is still where a message belongs.
usage_exit:
    sys_write 1, usage, usage_end - usage
    sys_exit 1
mem_exit:
    sys_write 1, memmsg, memmsg_end - memmsg
    sys_exit 1
big_exit:
    sys_write 1, bigmsg, bigmsg_end - bigmsg
    sys_exit 1
win_exit:
    sys_write 1, winmsg, winmsg_end - winmsg
    sys_exit 1


; ==========================
;   Data
; ==========================
usage:
    dstr "usage: editor <file>\n"
usage_end:
memmsg:
    dstr "editor: out of memory\n"
memmsg_end:
bigmsg:
    dstr "editor: file too large to edit\n"
bigmsg_end:
winmsg:
    dstr "editor: cannot create a window\n"
winmsg_end:

msg_title:
    dstr "editor  \0"
msg_mod:
    dstr "  *modified*\0"
msg_bsave:                           ; the buttons, all six glyphs wide: button_hit
    dstr "[Save]\0"                  ; places a press by dividing, so they must be
msg_bopen:                           ; the same width and in this order
    dstr "[Open]\0"
msg_bquit:
    dstr "[Quit]\0"
msg_open:
    dstr "open: \0"
msg_ln:
    dstr "ln \0"
msg_col:
    dstr ", col \0"
msg_new:
    dstr "new file\0"
msg_opened:
    dstr "opened\0"
msg_saved:
    dstr "saved\0"
msg_wfail:
    dstr "write failed\0"
msg_full:
    dstr "buffer full\0"
msg_toobig:
    dstr "file too large\0"
msg_confirm:
    dstr "unsaved changes - ESC again to quit\0"
msg_discard:
    dstr "unsaved changes - Enter again to load\0"

confpath:                            ; the settings, and the keys they are under
    dstr "/conf/editor.conf\0"
key_lineno:
    dstr "linenumbers\0"
key_wrap:
    dstr "wrap\0"

font:
    dfile "../../build/font.bin"     ; the same 8x16 blob the console uses


; ---- state ------------------------------------------------------------------
g_argc:
    d32 0
g_argv:
    d32 0
path:
    d32 0                            ; the file we are editing: argv[1] to begin with,
                                     ; and pathbuf once the open prompt has been used
prevpath:
    d32 0                            ; what it was, while a load is being attempted
sid:
    d32 0                            ; our surface
winbuf:
    d32 0                            ; the pixels we commit into it
textptr:
    d32 0                            ; the text buffer, behind the pixels
tlen:
    d32 0                            ; bytes of text
cur:
    d32 0                            ; the cursor, as a byte offset
anchor:
    d32 -1                           ; the other end of the selection, -1 = none
goalcol:
    d32 0                            ; the column up/down are aiming for
kmods:
    d32 0                            ; modifiers on the key being handled, and where
precur:
    d32 0                            ; the cursor was before it: what SHIFT+move needs
curline:
    d32 0                            ; where the cursor is, from measure
curcol:
    d32 0
nlines:
    d32 1
topline:
    d32 0                            ; the view
leftcol:
    d32 0
winw:
    d32 WANT_W                       ; the size we are currently drawing at
winh:
    d32 WANT_H
lastcfg:
    d32 0                            ; last config counter we adopted
totcols:
    d32 50                           ; character cells across the whole window,
cols:                                ; and the ones left for text once the gutter
    d32 50                           ; has taken its share
rows:
    d32 18                           ; (WANT_W / GW, WANT_H / GH)
textrows:
    d32 16
shownums:
    d32 0                            ; the settings, off until /conf/editor.conf
wrapmode:                            ; says otherwise - the editor there was before
    d32 0                            ; the file existed
gutcols:
    d32 0                            ; how wide the line-number column is, in cells
rownum:
    d32 0                            ; the number the next wrapped row carries, -1
rowsleft:                            ; for a continuation, and rows still to draw
    d32 0                            ; for the line being drawn
hitle:
    d32 0                            ; the line end hit_offset is walking past
confdest:
    d32 0                            ; the setting the line being parsed names
modified:
    d32 0                            ; the buffer differs from the file
dirty:
    d32 1                            ; the buffer differs from what we committed
quitasked:
    d32 0                            ; ESC pressed once with changes outstanding
quitreq:
    d32 0                            ; and the answer the main loop acts on
prompting:
    d32 0                            ; 1 while the open prompt has the keyboard
loadasked:
    d32 0                            ; Enter pressed once with changes outstanding
plen:
    d32 0                            ; characters in the line being typed
btnx:
    d32 236                          ; the button group's left edge, WANT_W - the span
btnshow:                             ; and its margin: layout redoes both on a resize
    d32 1                            ; 0 when the window is too narrow for them
statusmsg:
    d32 msg_new
tabn:
    d32 0                            ; spaces left to insert for a Tab
selstart:
    d32 -1                           ; the selection, resolved once per frame
selend:
    d32 -1
prevbtn:
    d32 0                            ; last frame's buttons, for press-edge detection
pbuf:
    res8 PATH_MAX                    ; the name being typed at the open prompt
pathbuf:
    res8 PATH_MAX                    ; and the one adopted from it, since argv is
                                     ; not ours to write over
cfgbuf:
    res8 12                          ; wconfig: width, height, config counter
inbuf:
    res8 12                          ; winput: x, y, buttons
numbuf:
    res8 16
confbuf:
    res8 CONFBUF_MAX                 ; /conf/editor.conf, read once and parsed in place
; fill_rect inputs
fx:
    d32 0
fy:
    d32 0
fw:
    d32 0
fh:
    d32 0
fc:
    d32 0
; draw_glyph inputs
gx:
    d32 0
gy:
    d32 0
gch:
    d32 0
gc:
    d32 0
gsrc:
    d32 0
